export const B2 = [
  {"id":"b2-01","level":"B2","icon":"🗳️","title":"How the country is run","grammar":"The passive with modal verbs, and ways around the passive","summary":"Modal + passive: the modal is conjugated, participle + worden go to the end in either order (moet worden goedgekeurd / goedgekeurd worden). Ways around it: men, zijn te, vallen te, zich laten, krijgen + participle.","explain":"You know the passive from units B1 · 10 and B1 · 11: `Het plan wordt besproken`. In the news and in government letters it is often combined with a modal verb: something **must**, **can** or **should have been** done. Dutch also has several ways around the passive, and a good writer knows when to use which.\n\n### A modal verb with the passive\n\nThe modal verb is the conjugated verb and stands in second place. `worden` becomes an infinitive and goes to the end together with the participle. There is **no te** after a modal verb.\n\n| tense | example |\n| present | Het voorstel moet worden goedgekeurd. |\n| past | Het voorstel moest worden goedgekeurd. |\n| perfect | Het voorstel heeft moeten worden goedgekeurd. |\n| past perfect | Het voorstel had moeten worden goedgekeurd. |\n| zou | Het voorstel zou kunnen worden goedgekeurd. |\n\n> De wet had al lang moeten worden aangepast. — The law should have been changed long ago.\n> Dit probleem zou door de gemeente kunnen worden opgelost. — This problem could be solved by the municipality.\n\nIn the perfect the modal takes **hebben** and is an infinitive, not a participle (unit B1 · 26): `heeft moeten worden`, never *heeft gemoeten worden. `had moeten worden` + participle means **should have been**: it did not happen. `had kunnen worden` means **could have been**.\n\n### The order of the verbs at the end\n\nThe participle may stand **before or after** `worden`: `moet worden goedgekeurd` and `moet goedgekeurd worden` are both standard Dutch, in writing and in speech.\n\nWith three verbs the participle stands either **last** or **first** in the group: `had moeten worden aangepast` or `had aangepast moeten worden`. In the middle (*had moeten aangepast worden) it is heard mainly in Belgium; avoid it. In a sub-clause the conjugated modal joins the group at the end, with the same two orders, and nothing else may come between the verbs:\n\n> Ik lees dat de wet moet worden aangepast. — I read that the law must be changed.\n> Ik lees dat de wet aangepast moet worden. — I read that the law must be changed.\n\n### The passive without a subject\n\nFormal texts use `er` + passive: `Er wordt verwacht dat …` (it is expected), `Er is besloten om …` (it has been decided to), `Er moet worden gestemd` (a vote must be held). In newspaper style the participle can even come first, without `er`: `Verwacht wordt dat …`, `Besloten is om …`.\n\n> Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag aftreedt. — It is expected that the minister will resign today.\n\n### Ways around the passive\n\n- **men** (formal): `Men verwacht dat …` = it is expected. In speech `je`, `ze` or `we`: `Ze gaan de belasting verhogen` (they, the authorities).\n- **zijn + te + infinitive** = can be done: `De uitslag is op de website te vinden`, `Dat is niet te doen`. In official letters it can mean *must be*: `Het bedrag is binnen dertig dagen te betalen`. A separable verb takes te in the middle: `in te zien`.\n- **vallen te**, mostly with niets, weinig, veel: `Er valt niets te zeggen`.\n- **zich laten** + infinitive (formal) = can be done: `Dat laat zich makkelijk verklaren`.\n- **krijgen + participle**: the person who receives something is the subject. `Hij kreeg een boete opgelegd`, `Ze kregen het formulier toegestuurd`.\n\n> Aan de uitslag valt niets meer te veranderen. — Nothing more can be changed about the result.\n> De kiezers krijgen hun stempas thuis toegestuurd. — The voters are sent their polling card at home.\n\n### Passive or active?\n\nThe passive suits formal writing and a doer who is unknown, unimportant or obvious. Use the active when the doer is the news (`De gemeenteraad heeft het plan afgewezen`) and in conversation.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Do not forget worden: *Het plan moet goedgekeurd → `Het plan moet worden goedgekeurd`.\n- hebben in the perfect: *De wet is moeten worden aangepast → `De wet heeft moeten worden aangepast`.\n- No te after a modal: *Het moet te worden betaald → `Het moet worden betaald`. Only `hoeven` takes te: `Het hoeft niet te worden betaald` (unit B2 · 14).\n- The receiver is not the subject of worden: *De kiezers worden een brief gestuurd → `De kiezers krijgen een brief gestuurd`, or formally `Aan de kiezers wordt een brief gestuurd`.\n- `moet worden goedgekeurd` is a process still to come; `moet zijn goedgekeurd` means it must be done by a certain moment.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can say `вирішено` or `кажуть, що …` with no subject at all. Dutch needs something in that slot: `Er is besloten`, `Men zegt dat …`, `Ze zeggen dat …`.","words":[{"nl":"verkiezing","art":"de","pl":"verkiezingen","en":"election","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kabinet","art":"het","pl":"kabinetten","en":"cabinet (the government)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wetsvoorstel","art":"het","pl":"wetsvoorstellen","en":"bill (a proposed law)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"coalitie","art":"de","pl":"coalities","en":"coalition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"oppositie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"opposition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kiezer","art":"de","pl":"kiezers","en":"voter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stempas","art":"de","pl":"stempassen","en":"polling card","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitslag","art":"de","pl":"uitslagen","en":"result (of a vote or test)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"maatregel","art":"de","pl":"maatregelen","en":"measure (a step taken)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"begroting","art":"de","pl":"begrotingen","en":"budget","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gemeenteraad","art":"de","pl":"gemeenteraden","en":"municipal council","pos":"n"},{"nl":"goedkeuren","past":"keurde goed","perf":"heeft goedgekeurd","en":"to approve","pos":"v"},{"nl":"invoeren","past":"voerde in","perf":"heeft ingevoerd","en":"to introduce (a rule or system)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afschaffen","past":"schafte af","perf":"heeft afgeschaft","en":"to abolish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aftreden","past":"trad af","perf":"is afgetreden","en":"to resign (from office)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aannemen","past":"nam aan","perf":"heeft aangenomen","en":"to pass (a law) / to accept","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Het wetsvoorstel moet door beide Kamers worden goedgekeurd.","en":"The bill has to be approved by both Houses (of parliament).","hint":["wetsvoorstel","moeten","beide","Kamer","worden","goedkeuren"],"note":"Moet is the conjugated verb in second place; worden and the participle goedgekeurd go to the end, in either order.","alt":["Het wetsvoorstel moet door beide Kamers goedgekeurd worden."]},{"nl":"Volgens de oppositie had de begroting veel eerder moeten worden aangepast.","en":"According to the opposition the budget should have been adjusted much earlier.","hint":["volgens","oppositie","begroting","eerder","moeten","aanpassen"],"note":"Should have been = had moeten worden + participle: it did not happen. Moeten is an infinitive here, not a participle, and the participle may also stand first: aangepast moeten worden.","alt":["Volgens de oppositie had de begroting veel eerder aangepast moeten worden.","De begroting had volgens de oppositie veel eerder moeten worden aangepast.","De begroting had volgens de oppositie veel eerder aangepast moeten worden."]},{"nl":"De kiezers willen weten of de maatregel kan worden afgeschaft.","en":"The voters want to know whether the measure can be abolished.","hint":["kiezer","willen","weten","maatregel","kunnen","afschaffen"],"note":"In the of-clause the conjugated kan joins the verb group at the end. The participle stands last or first in the group: kan worden afgeschaft or afgeschaft kan worden.","alt":["De kiezers willen weten of de maatregel afgeschaft kan worden."]},{"nl":"Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag aftreedt.","en":"It is expected that the minister will resign today.","hint":["verwachten","minister","vandaag","aftreden"],"note":"A passive without a subject: er fills the first place. Aftreden is not passive itself; in the dat-clause the separable verb stays in one piece at the end: aftreedt.","alt":["Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag aftreedt.","Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag zal aftreden.","Er wordt verwacht dat de minister vandaag af zal treden.","Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag zal aftreden.","Verwacht wordt dat de minister vandaag af zal treden."]},{"nl":"Er is besloten om de verkiezingen een maand uit te stellen.","en":"It has been decided to postpone the elections by a month.","hint":["besluiten","verkiezing","maand","uitstellen"],"note":"Er is besloten: the impersonal passive in the perfect, typical of official texts. Om may be left out; uit te stellen has te between the prefix and the verb.","alt":["Er is besloten de verkiezingen een maand uit te stellen."]},{"nl":"Men zegt dat het kabinet binnenkort zal vallen.","en":"It is said that the cabinet will fall soon. (with men)","hint":["men","zeggen","kabinet","binnenkort","vallen"],"note":"Men replaces a passive (Er wordt gezegd dat …) and takes a singular verb: men zegt. It is formal; in speech you would say ze zeggen.","alt":["Men zegt dat het kabinet binnenkort valt."]},{"nl":"De uitslag van de verkiezingen is vanaf morgen op de website te vinden.","en":"The result of the elections can be found on the website from tomorrow. (with zijn te)","hint":["uitslag","verkiezing","vanaf","morgen","website","vinden"],"note":"zijn + te + infinitive means 'can be done': is te vinden = can be found. It replaces kan worden gevonden.","alt":["Vanaf morgen is de uitslag van de verkiezingen op de website te vinden."]},{"nl":"Aan de uitslag valt niets meer te veranderen.","en":"Nothing more can be changed about the result. (with vallen te)","hint":["uitslag","vallen","niets","meer","veranderen"],"note":"vallen te + infinitive means 'can be done', mostly with niets, weinig or veel. Valt stays singular, and the infinitive veranderen goes to the end with te.","alt":["Er valt aan de uitslag niets meer te veranderen.","Er valt niets meer aan de uitslag te veranderen."]},{"nl":"De winst van de oppositie laat zich makkelijk verklaren.","en":"The opposition's win can easily be explained. (with zich laten)","hint":["winst","oppositie","zich laten","makkelijk","verklaren"],"note":"zich laten + infinitive is a formal way of saying 'can be done': laat zich verklaren = kan worden verklaard. Zich refers back to the subject, de winst.","alt":["De winst van de oppositie laat zich gemakkelijk verklaren."]},{"nl":"Alle kiezers krijgen hun stempas thuis toegestuurd.","en":"All voters are sent their polling card at home. (with krijgen)","hint":["kiezer","krijgen","stempas","thuis","toesturen"],"note":"krijgen + participle makes the receiver the subject. With worden that is impossible: never *De kiezers worden hun stempas toegestuurd.","alt":["Alle kiezers krijgen thuis hun stempas toegestuurd."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het wetsvoorstel moet eerst door de Tweede Kamer worden ___.","options":["goedkeuren","goedgekeurd","goedgekeurt","te goedkeuren"],"correct":1,"en":"The bill must first be approved by the House of Representatives.","note":"After worden comes the participle: goedgekeurd. It ends in d, because keuren has the stem keur (not in 't kofschip)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Vanwege de storm ___ de verkiezingen moeten worden uitgesteld.","options":["zijn","hebben","werden"],"correct":1,"en":"Because of the storm the elections have had to be postponed.","note":"The perfect of a modal verb is always formed with hebben: hebben moeten. That stays so in the passive: *zijn moeten worden is wrong."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Alle leden van de gemeenteraad ___ het rapport vooraf toegestuurd.","options":["worden","zijn","krijgen"],"correct":2,"en":"All members of the municipal council are sent the report in advance.","note":"The members receive the report, so krijgen + participle. Dutch cannot make the receiver the subject of worden: *De leden worden het rapport toegestuurd."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het plan hoeft niet door de gemeenteraad ___ worden goedgekeurd.","options":["–","te","om te"],"correct":1,"en":"The plan does not need to be approved by the municipal council.","note":"Hoeven is the one modal-like verb that takes te: hoeft niet te worden. After moeten, kunnen, mogen and willen there is no te."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De maatregel zal waarschijnlijk worden ___ (afschaffen).","answers":["afgeschaft"],"en":"The measure will probably be abolished.","note":"zal … worden + participle. Afschaffen: ge comes after the prefix, and schaf ends in f, so the participle ends in t: afgeschaft."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Er ___ (worden) verwacht dat de coalitie zetels verliest.","answers":["wordt"],"en":"It is expected that the coalition will lose seats.","note":"The impersonal passive has er in first place and a singular verb: er wordt. What is expected follows in the dat-clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tijdens de crisis ___ (moeten) veel maatregelen snel worden ingevoerd.","answers":["moesten"],"en":"During the crisis many measures had to be introduced quickly.","note":"The subject is veel maatregelen, plural, and the story is in the past: moesten. Worden and ingevoerd stay at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De uitslag ___ (laten) zich moeilijk voorspellen.","answers":["laat"],"en":"The result is hard to predict.","note":"zich laten + infinitive: can be done. De uitslag is singular: laat, stem without t because the stem already ends in t."},{"type":"order","lead":"De oppositie vindt","words":["dat","de begroting","moet","worden","aangepast"],"answers":["De oppositie vindt dat de begroting moet worden aangepast.","De oppositie vindt dat de begroting aangepast moet worden."],"en":"The opposition thinks that the budget must be adjusted.","note":"In the sub-clause all three verbs stand together at the end. The participle stands last (moet worden aangepast) or first (aangepast moet worden); both are standard."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgens de krant","words":["had","het wetsvoorstel","veel eerder","moeten","worden","aangenomen"],"answers":["Volgens de krant had het wetsvoorstel veel eerder moeten worden aangenomen.","Volgens de krant had het wetsvoorstel veel eerder aangenomen moeten worden."],"en":"According to the newspaper the bill should have been passed much earlier.","note":"Volgens de krant is first, so had comes second and the subject follows. Should have been = had moeten worden + participle; the participle goes last or first in the group."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dit probleem","words":["zou","door de gemeenteraad","kunnen","worden","opgelost"],"answers":["Dit probleem zou door de gemeenteraad kunnen worden opgelost.","Dit probleem zou door de gemeenteraad opgelost kunnen worden.","Dit probleem zou kunnen worden opgelost door de gemeenteraad.","Dit probleem zou opgelost kunnen worden door de gemeenteraad."],"en":"This problem could be solved by the municipal council.","note":"Zou is conjugated and stands second; kunnen, worden and the participle form the group at the end. The door-phrase normally stands before the group, but may also follow it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it passive and leave out the doer. Keep the modal verb.","from":"De regering moet de wet aanpassen.","answers":["De wet moet worden aangepast.","De wet moet aangepast worden."],"en":"The law must be changed.","note":"The object de wet becomes the subject. Moet stays in second place; aanpassen becomes worden + aangepast at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with zijn + te + infinitive.","from":"Een nieuwe stempas kan op het stadhuis worden aangevraagd.","answers":["Een nieuwe stempas is op het stadhuis aan te vragen.","Een nieuwe stempas is aan te vragen op het stadhuis."],"en":"A new polling card can be applied for at the town hall.","note":"kan worden + participle becomes is te + infinitive. Aanvragen is separable, so te goes between the prefix and the verb: aan te vragen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that it should have happened but did not: use had moeten.","from":"Het plan moet eerder worden besproken.","answers":["Het plan had eerder moeten worden besproken.","Het plan had eerder besproken moeten worden."],"en":"The plan should have been discussed earlier.","note":"Had takes the second place, moeten becomes an infinitive in the group at the end. Not *had gemoeten: with another verb the modal is an infinitive."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De wet is moeten worden aangepast.","answers":["De wet heeft moeten worden aangepast.","De wet heeft aangepast moeten worden."],"en":"The law has had to be changed.","note":"The perfect of moeten takes hebben, also when worden follows. The is of an ordinary passive perfect (is aangepast) does not apply here."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De kiezers werden een brief toegestuurd.","answers":["De kiezers kregen een brief toegestuurd.","De kiezers werd een brief toegestuurd.","Aan de kiezers werd een brief toegestuurd.","Er werd de kiezers een brief toegestuurd."],"en":"The voters were sent a letter.","note":"The receiver cannot be the subject of worden. Make it the subject of krijgen (kregen … toegestuurd), or keep een brief as the subject: then the verb is singular, werd."}]},
  {"id":"b2-05","level":"B2","icon":"📄","title":"Official letters","grammar":"Noun style: het + infinitive, -ing, and turning it back into verbs","summary":"Formal Dutch turns actions into nouns: het + infinitive (het indienen van een aanvraag), -ing nouns, always de (de betaling), and phrases like na ontvangst van. The object follows van.","explain":"Official letters are full of nouns where everyday Dutch would use a verb: `Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag …` instead of `Nadat wij uw aanvraag hebben ontvangen …`. This is called noun style (*de naamwoordstijl*). You need to read it, write it where a formal text asks for it, and turn it back into verbs.\n\n### 1. het + infinitive\n\nAlmost any verb becomes a noun when you put `het` in front of the infinitive. It is **always a het-word** and has no plural. The object of the verb follows with `van`; the doer, if it is mentioned at all, follows with `door`.\n\n> Het invullen van het formulier duurt ongeveer tien minuten. — Filling in the form takes about ten minutes.\n> Bij het indienen van een bezwaar moet u een kopie van het besluit meesturen. — When submitting an objection you must enclose a copy of the decision.\n\n`bij het …` means \"when …, while …\", `na het …` \"after …\", `voor het …` \"before …\". A separable verb stays in one word: `het indienen`, `het intrekken`.\n\nAn adverb that goes with the verb may stay an adverb or become an adjective with -e: `het snel groeien` and `het snelle groeien` van de stad are both standard Dutch. Official letters usually keep the form without -e, especially with a phrase like `te laat`:\n\n> Het tijdig indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid. — Submitting the application on time is your own responsibility.\n\n### 2. Nouns in -ing and other action nouns\n\nMany verbs make a noun with stem + `-ing`:\n\n| verb | noun |\n| betalen | de betaling |\n| behandelen | de behandeling |\n| verlengen | de verlenging |\n| afwijzen | de afwijzing |\n| indienen | de indiening |\n\n**Every -ing noun is a de-word**, with the plural in -en: `de betalingen`. Not every verb has an -ing noun. Many have a noun of their own, and then that is the one to use; its article has to be learned: `ontvangen → de ontvangst`, `aanvragen → de aanvraag`, `besluiten → het besluit`, `vertrekken → het vertrek`, `uitstellen → het uitstel`.\n\n> De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt ongeveer acht weken. — The processing of your application takes about eight weeks.\n\n### 3. A preposition and a noun instead of a sub-clause\n\n| noun style | with a verb |\n| na ontvangst van uw brief | nadat wij uw brief hebben ontvangen |\n| bij vertrek | als u vertrekt |\n| wegens ziekte | omdat iemand ziek is |\n| ter voorkoming van fraude | om fraude te voorkomen |\n| in afwachting van het besluit | terwijl u op het besluit wacht |\n| op verzoek van uw werkgever | omdat uw werkgever erom heeft gevraagd |\n\nSeveral have no article: `na ontvangst`, `bij vertrek`. `ter` is an old form that only survives in fixed phrases (more in B2 · 21). `wegens` is a preposition: it takes a noun, never a sub-clause.\n\n> Wegens ziekte gaat het spreekuur vandaag niet door. — Because of illness the surgery hours are cancelled today.\n\n### Word order\n\nThe whole phrase counts as **one element**. When it stands first, the conjugated verb follows it directly, and then the subject.\n\n> Na ontvangst van uw brief nemen wij contact met u op. — After receipt of your letter we will contact you.\n\n### Reading it: find the action and the doer\n\nNoun style hides who does what, and when. Find the verb inside the noun (`behandeling → behandelen`) and the doer (often the authority), then say it with a verb:\n\n- `de behandeling van uw aanvraag` → wij behandelen uw aanvraag\n- `wegens te late indiening` → omdat u de aanvraag te laat hebt ingediend\n\n`te laat` becomes `te late` because it now stands before a de-noun.\n\n### Writing it yourself\n\nVerbs are clearer, so use them where you can; noun style fits forms and formal letters. Avoid chains of `van`: `de datum van de behandeling van de aanvraag van uw werkgever` is correct but hard to read; `de datum waarop wij de aanvraag van uw werkgever behandelen` is better.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **het** with the infinitive, **de** with -ing: `het verlengen`, `de verlenging`; never *het verlenging or *de verlengen.\n- The object needs `van`: `het intrekken van uw aanvraag`, not *het intrekken uw aanvraag.\n- After the phrase at the start comes the verb: `Na ontvangst van uw brief hebben wij …`, not *Na ontvangst van uw brief wij hebben …\n- `wegens` + noun, `omdat` + sub-clause: `Omdat ik ziek was, …`, never *Wegens ik ziek was, …\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian official style does the same: `подання заяви`, `розгляд заяви`. Where Ukrainian puts the object in the genitive, Dutch uses `van`: `het indienen van een aanvraag`, `de behandeling van de aanvraag`.","words":[{"nl":"aanvraag","art":"de","pl":"aanvragen","en":"application (for a permit, benefit)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"indienen","past":"diende in","perf":"heeft ingediend","en":"to submit / to file","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"ontvangst","art":"de","pl":"","en":"receipt (of a letter, payment)","pos":"n","tag":"no plural in this sense"},{"nl":"behandeling","art":"de","pl":"behandelingen","en":"processing (of a case) / treatment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verlenging","art":"de","pl":"verlengingen","en":"extension / renewal","pos":"n"},{"nl":"termijn","art":"de","pl":"termijnen","en":"period allowed / time limit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"besluit","art":"het","pl":"besluiten","en":"decision","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezwaar","art":"het","pl":"bezwaren","en":"objection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afwijzing","art":"de","pl":"afwijzingen","en":"rejection / refusal","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitstel","art":"het","pl":"","en":"postponement / extra time","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"vaststellen","past":"stelde vast","perf":"heeft vastgesteld","en":"to fix / to determine","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verstrekken","past":"verstrekte","perf":"heeft verstrekt","en":"to provide (information)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"intrekken","past":"trok in","perf":"heeft ingetrokken","en":"to withdraw (an application)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"tijdig","en":"timely / in time","pos":"adj","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"wegens","en":"because of / owing to","pos":"prep","tag":"formal"},{"nl":"in afwachting van","en":"pending / awaiting","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag sturen wij u binnen twee weken een bevestiging.","en":"After receipt of your application we will send you a confirmation within two weeks.","hint":["na","ontvangst","aanvraag","sturen","bevestiging"],"note":"Na ontvangst van stands for nadat wij … hebben ontvangen. The whole phrase is the first element, so the verb sturen follows directly and then wij.","alt":["Wij sturen u na ontvangst van uw aanvraag binnen twee weken een bevestiging."]},{"nl":"Bij het indienen van een bezwaar moet u een kopie van het besluit meesturen.","en":"When submitting an objection you must enclose a copy of the decision.","hint":["bij","indienen","bezwaar","moeten","kopie","meesturen"],"note":"het + infinitive: het indienen, always a het-word. Its object, een bezwaar, follows with van. Bij het … means \"when …\".","alt":["U moet bij het indienen van een bezwaar een kopie van het besluit meesturen."]},{"nl":"Wegens ziekte heb ik niet binnen de termijn kunnen reageren.","en":"Because of illness I was not able to respond within the time limit.","hint":["wegens","ziekte","termijn","kunnen","reageren"],"note":"Wegens is a preposition and takes a noun without an article: wegens ziekte, the noun-style version of omdat ik ziek was.","alt":["Wegens ziekte kon ik niet binnen de termijn reageren.","Ik heb wegens ziekte niet binnen de termijn kunnen reageren.","Ik kon wegens ziekte niet binnen de termijn reageren."]},{"nl":"De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt langer als u niet alle gegevens verstrekt.","en":"The processing of your application takes longer if you do not provide all the details.","hint":["behandeling","aanvraag","duren","gegevens","verstrekken"],"note":"behandelen → de behandeling: an -ing noun is always de. The action is behandelen and the doer, the authority, is not mentioned.","alt":["De behandeling van uw aanvraag duurt langer wanneer u niet alle gegevens verstrekt."]},{"nl":"Het tijdig indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid.","en":"Submitting the application on time is your own responsibility.","hint":["tijdig","indienen","aanvraag","eigen","verantwoordelijkheid"],"note":"tijdig goes with the verb inside het indienen. Official texts keep it without -e; het tijdige indienen is also correct.","alt":["Het tijdige indienen van de aanvraag is uw eigen verantwoordelijkheid."]},{"nl":"Op verzoek van uw werkgever hebben wij de termijn met een maand verlengd.","en":"At the request of your employer we have extended the time limit by one month.","hint":["op verzoek van","werkgever","termijn","maand","verlengen"],"note":"Op verzoek van uw werkgever replaces a clause like omdat uw werkgever erom heeft gevraagd. It is one element, so hebben comes straight after it.","alt":["Wij hebben de termijn op verzoek van uw werkgever met een maand verlengd."]},{"nl":"In afwachting van ons besluit mag u in Nederland blijven.","en":"Pending our decision you may stay in the Netherlands.","hint":["in afwachting van","besluit","mogen","Nederland","blijven"],"note":"In afwachting van packs \"while you wait for\" into a noun phrase. It stands first, so the verb mag follows and then u.","alt":["U mag in afwachting van ons besluit in Nederland blijven.","U mag in Nederland blijven in afwachting van ons besluit."]},{"nl":"Omdat u het formulier te laat hebt ingediend, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","en":"Because you submitted the form too late, your application is being rejected.","hint":["omdat","formulier","te laat","indienen","aanvraag","afwijzen"],"note":"This is the verb version of wegens te late indiening: the doer (u) and the tense are visible again. Hebt or heeft are both correct with u.","alt":["Omdat u het formulier te laat heeft ingediend, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Omdat u het formulier te laat ingediend hebt, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Omdat u het formulier te laat ingediend heeft, wordt uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Uw aanvraag wordt afgewezen omdat u het formulier te laat hebt ingediend.","Uw aanvraag wordt afgewezen omdat u het formulier te laat heeft ingediend."]},{"nl":"Wegens te late indiening is uw aanvraag afgewezen.","en":"Because of late submission your application has been rejected.","hint":["wegens","te laat","indiening","aanvraag","afwijzen"],"note":"indienen → de indiening. Te laat now stands before a de-noun, so it becomes an adjective with -e: te late indiening.","alt":["Uw aanvraag is wegens te late indiening afgewezen.","Wegens late indiening is uw aanvraag afgewezen.","Uw aanvraag is wegens late indiening afgewezen."]},{"nl":"Na het ontvangen van het besluit hebt u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","en":"After receiving the decision you have six weeks to lodge an objection.","hint":["na","ontvangen","besluit","tijd","bezwaar maken"],"note":"het ontvangen van het besluit: het + infinitive with the object after van. Na ontvangst van het besluit says the same with the noun of ontvangen.","alt":["Na het ontvangen van het besluit heeft u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","Na ontvangst van het besluit hebt u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","Na ontvangst van het besluit heeft u zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","U hebt na het ontvangen van het besluit zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken.","U heeft na het ontvangen van het besluit zes weken de tijd om bezwaar te maken."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ invullen van het formulier kost ongeveer tien minuten.","options":["De","Het","Een"],"correct":1,"en":"Filling in the form takes about ten minutes.","note":"An infinitive used as a noun is always a het-word: het invullen. De goes with -ing nouns, not with an infinitive."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De ___ van uw verblijfsvergunning is alleen mogelijk op afspraak.","options":["verlengen","verleng","verlenging","verlengd"],"correct":2,"en":"The renewal of your residence permit is only possible by appointment.","note":"With de in front only the -ing noun fits: de verlenging. The infinitive would need het: het verlengen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na ___ van uw brief nemen wij contact met u op.","options":["ontvangen","ontvangst","ontvanging","het ontvangst"],"correct":1,"en":"After receipt of your letter we will contact you.","note":"ontvangen has its own noun, de ontvangst; there is no ontvanging. Ontvangen without het cannot stand after na, and ontvangst is a de-word."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ ziekte gaat de cursus deze week niet door.","options":["Omdat","Wegens","Doordat"],"correct":1,"en":"Because of illness the course is not going ahead this week.","note":"Ziekte is a noun, so a preposition is needed: wegens. Omdat and doordat are conjunctions and need a full sub-clause with a verb."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tegen de ___ (afwijzen) van uw aanvraag kunt u bezwaar maken.","answers":["afwijzing"],"en":"You can lodge an objection against the rejection of your application.","note":"De in front asks for the -ing noun: afwijzen → de afwijzing. Het afwijzen would need het."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ___ (wijzigen) van uw adres moet u binnen vijf dagen doorgeven.","answers":["wijziging"],"en":"You must report the change of your address within five days.","note":"wijzigen → de wijziging. Every -ing noun is a de-word, which matches the de in front."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na ___ (betalen) van het bedrag ontvangt u een bevestiging.","answers":["betaling","het betalen","de betaling"],"en":"After payment of the amount you will receive a confirmation.","note":"Both noun forms are correct: na betaling (the usual fixed phrase, no article), na de betaling, or na het betalen. Na betalen without het is not."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (snel) behandelen van aanvragen is ons belangrijkste doel.","answers":["snel","snelle"],"en":"Processing applications quickly is our most important aim.","note":"An adverb with het + infinitive may stay an adverb (het snel behandelen) or become an adjective with -e (het snelle behandelen). Both are standard."},{"type":"order","lead":"Na ontvangst van uw brief","words":["nemen","wij","zo snel mogelijk","contact met u","op"],"answers":["Na ontvangst van uw brief nemen wij zo snel mogelijk contact met u op."],"en":"After receipt of your letter we will contact you as soon as possible.","note":"The noun phrase at the start is one element, so the verb nemen comes second and wij third. The particle op closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Wegens financiële problemen","words":["heeft","zij","uitstel van betaling","aangevraagd"],"answers":["Wegens financiële problemen heeft zij uitstel van betaling aangevraagd."],"en":"Because of financial problems she has applied for a postponement of payment.","note":"Wegens + noun phrase is the first element; heeft follows directly, then zij. Uitstel van betaling is itself noun style: she wants to pay later."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the sub-clause with na + the noun of ontvangen.","from":"Nadat wij uw aanvraag hebben ontvangen, sturen wij u een bevestiging.","answers":["Na ontvangst van uw aanvraag sturen wij u een bevestiging.","Wij sturen u na ontvangst van uw aanvraag een bevestiging."],"en":"After receipt of your application we will send you a confirmation.","note":"ontvangen → de ontvangst, with no article in this fixed phrase. The doer wij disappears from the phrase, and the verb sturen comes straight after it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the omdat-clause with wegens + an -ing noun.","from":"Omdat de aanvraag te laat is ingediend, wordt deze niet behandeld.","answers":["Wegens te late indiening wordt de aanvraag niet behandeld.","De aanvraag wordt wegens te late indiening niet behandeld.","Wegens de te late indiening wordt de aanvraag niet behandeld."],"en":"Because of late submission the application is not being processed.","note":"indienen → de indiening, and te laat becomes te late before the noun. The noun aanvraag now has to be named in the main clause."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn the noun phrase into a sub-clause with omdat.","from":"Wegens het ontbreken van uw handtekening kunnen wij uw aanvraag niet behandelen.","answers":["Omdat uw handtekening ontbreekt, kunnen wij uw aanvraag niet behandelen.","Wij kunnen uw aanvraag niet behandelen, omdat uw handtekening ontbreekt."],"en":"Because your signature is missing, we cannot process your application.","note":"The action is ontbreken and its subject is uw handtekening, which was hidden behind van. In the sub-clause the verb goes to the end: ontbreekt."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Write it with verbs: use wij and nadat.","from":"De vaststelling van het bedrag vindt plaats na ontvangst van alle gegevens.","answers":["Wij stellen het bedrag vast nadat wij alle gegevens hebben ontvangen.","Wij stellen het bedrag vast nadat wij alle gegevens ontvangen hebben.","Nadat wij alle gegevens hebben ontvangen, stellen wij het bedrag vast.","Nadat wij alle gegevens ontvangen hebben, stellen wij het bedrag vast."],"en":"We determine the amount after we have received all the details.","note":"vaststelling → vaststellen, ontvangst → ontvangen. With verbs the doer (wij) appears. At the end of the sub-clause both hebben ontvangen and ontvangen hebben are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Na ontvangst van uw brief wij hebben uw gegevens gecontroleerd.","answers":["Na ontvangst van uw brief hebben wij uw gegevens gecontroleerd."],"en":"After receipt of your letter we checked your details.","note":"Na ontvangst van uw brief is the first element, so the conjugated verb hebben must come second, before the subject wij."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het intrekken uw aanvraag is kosteloos.","answers":["Het intrekken van uw aanvraag is kosteloos."],"en":"Withdrawing your application is free of charge.","note":"With het + infinitive the object cannot follow directly; it needs van: het intrekken van uw aanvraag."}]},
  {"id":"b2-09","level":"B2","icon":"🎙️","title":"Media and journalism","grammar":"Reported speech: tenses, zou, and what is only claimed","summary":"After a past verb of saying the tense moves back (ik ben → hij was; ik heb → hij had) and the future becomes zou. zou also marks a claim that is not confirmed; volgens is followed by the verb.","explain":"You can quote someone's words (direct speech, *de directe rede*) or build them into a sub-clause with `dat`, `of` or a question word (indirect speech, *de indirecte rede*). In that sub-clause the verbs go to the end (unit B1 · 02). Journalists also have their own tools to show that something is only a claim.\n\n### Tenses after a past verb of saying\n\nWhen the verb of saying is in the present (`Hij zegt dat …`), the tense of the words stays as it was. When it is in the past (`zei`, `vertelde`, `meldde`), the tense usually moves one step back:\n\n| said | reported after zei dat … |\n| ik ben ziek | hij ziek **was** |\n| ik heb gebeld | hij **had** gebeld |\n| ik belde | hij **had** gebeld |\n| ik zal komen / ik kom morgen | hij **zou** komen |\n\n> De woordvoerder zei dat de minister ziek was. — The spokesperson said that the minister was ill.\n> De verslaggever vertelde dat hij drie keer had gebeld. — The reporter said that he had called three times.\n> Hij zei dat hij naar de persconferentie zou komen. — He said that he would come to the press conference.\n\nAt the end of the clause both orders of the two verbs are standard Dutch: `dat hij had gebeld` and `dat hij gebeld had`. With `zou` + infinitive, `zou komen` is the usual order; `komen zou` also occurs.\n\nThe present stays when the statement is still true or is a general fact. Dutch is less strict than English here, and in speech the present is heard more widely; in careful writing, move the tense back.\n\n> Hij zei dat Amsterdam de hoofdstad is. — He said that Amsterdam is the capital.\n\n### Person, time and place\n\nPersons move to the reporter's point of view (`ik` → `hij`/`zij`, `jij` → `ik`), and time and place words change when that moment or place lies behind you:\n\n- `vandaag` → `die dag`; `nu` → `toen`, `op dat moment`\n- `morgen` → `de volgende dag`, `de dag erna`; `gisteren` → `de dag ervoor`\n- `volgende week` → `de week erna`; `hier` → `daar`\n\n> Ze zei dat ze me de volgende dag zou bellen. — She said that she would call me the next day.\n\n### Questions and orders\n\nA yes/no question is reported with `of`; a question word stays. An order becomes `dat … moest`, or `vragen` + `(om) te` + infinitive.\n\n> De verslaggever vroeg of de minister zou aftreden. — The reporter asked whether the minister would resign.\n> De redactie zei dat ik moest wachten. — The editors said that I had to wait.\n> De redactie vroeg me te wachten. — The editors asked me to wait.\n\n### zou: only claimed\n\nIn the news, `zou` / `zouden` + infinitive also marks something as **unconfirmed**: the journalist passes it on without vouching for it (English *is said to*, *allegedly*). For the past: `zou hebben` / `zou zijn` + participle. So `De minister zou aftreden` is either \"was going to resign\" or \"is said to be resigning\": context decides.\n\n> Er zouden tien gewonden zijn. — There are said to be ten injured.\n> De burgemeester zou van de fraude hebben geweten. — The mayor allegedly knew about the fraud.\n\nOther ways to mark a claim:\n\n- `volgens` + source, often first, with the verb straight after it.\n- `naar verluidt` (reportedly), `naar eigen zeggen` (by his or her own account).\n- `beweren dat`, `stellen dat`.\n- `aldus` + source after a quotation or a summary of one, with no verb.\n- `schijnen te` (is said to): `Hij schijnt ziek te zijn` (more in B2 · 14).\n\n> Volgens de politie is de dader gevlucht. — According to the police the offender has fled.\n> De brand is onder controle, aldus de brandweer. — The fire is under control, according to the fire brigade.\n\n### Verbs of saying\n\n- `zeggen`, `vertellen`: neutral. `melden`: report news. `stellen`: state firmly.\n- `beweren`: claim; it hints that the claim may be untrue.\n- `benadrukken`: stress. `toegeven`: admit something unpleasant. `ontkennen`: deny.\n- `suggereren`: imply without saying it outright. To propose a plan is `voorstellen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- **Inversion after volgens**: `Volgens de politie is de man gevlucht`, not *Volgens de politie de man is gevlucht.\n- **of** for a yes/no question: `Hij vroeg of ik kwam`, not *Hij vroeg dat ik kwam or *als ik kwam.\n- **ontkennen already says no**: `Hij ontkende dat hij had gelogen` means he said he had not lied. A `niet` in the clause turns the meaning around.\n- **aldus takes no verb**: not *aldus zei de woordvoerder.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian keeps the tense of the original words: `Він сказав, що він хворий`. Dutch moves it back: `Hij zei dat hij ziek was`. The Ukrainian `чи` in a reported question is Dutch `of`.","words":[{"nl":"verslaggever","art":"de","pl":"verslaggevers","en":"reporter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"woordvoerder","art":"de","pl":"woordvoerders","en":"spokesperson","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bron","art":"de","pl":"bronnen","en":"source (of information)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"persbericht","art":"het","pl":"persberichten","en":"press release","pos":"n"},{"nl":"persconferentie","art":"de","pl":"persconferenties","en":"press conference","pos":"n"},{"nl":"redactie","art":"de","pl":"redacties","en":"editorial staff / newsroom","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bewering","art":"de","pl":"beweringen","en":"claim / assertion","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verklaring","art":"de","pl":"verklaringen","en":"statement (official)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beweren","past":"beweerde","perf":"heeft beweerd","en":"to claim","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontkennen","past":"ontkende","perf":"heeft ontkend","en":"to deny","pos":"v"},{"nl":"toegeven","past":"gaf toe","perf":"heeft toegegeven","en":"to admit","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"benadrukken","past":"benadrukte","perf":"heeft benadrukt","en":"to stress / to emphasise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"suggereren","past":"suggereerde","perf":"heeft gesuggereerd","en":"to imply / to hint","pos":"v"},{"nl":"anoniem","en":"anonymous","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"naar verluidt","en":"reportedly","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"aldus","en":"according to (after a quotation)","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Tijdens de persconferentie benadrukte de woordvoerder dat het onderzoek nog niet klaar was.","en":"During the press conference the spokesperson stressed that the investigation was not finished yet.","hint":["persconferentie","benadrukken","woordvoerder","onderzoek","klaar"],"note":"The words were 'Het onderzoek is nog niet klaar'; after the past verb benadrukte the present is moves back to was, at the end of the dat-clause.","alt":["De woordvoerder benadrukte tijdens de persconferentie dat het onderzoek nog niet klaar was."]},{"nl":"De verslaggever vertelde ons dat hij de minister al drie keer had gebeld.","en":"The reporter told us that he had already called the minister three times.","hint":["verslaggever","vertellen","minister","drie keer","bellen"],"note":"His words were 'Ik heb al drie keer gebeld': the perfect becomes the past perfect, had gebeld. Gebeld had is just as correct.","alt":["De verslaggever vertelde ons dat hij de minister al drie keer gebeld had."]},{"nl":"Onze docent zei dat een goede journalist altijd twee bronnen controleert.","en":"Our teacher said that a good journalist always checks two sources.","hint":["docent","zeggen","journalist","bron","controleren"],"note":"A general rule that is still true keeps the present after zei: controleert, not controleerde."},{"nl":"De minister beloofde dat hij de volgende dag zou reageren.","en":"The minister promised that he would respond the next day.","hint":["minister","beloven","volgende","dag","reageren"],"note":"He said 'Ik reageer morgen'. A future becomes zou + infinitive, and morgen becomes de volgende dag because that day has passed.","alt":["De minister beloofde dat hij de volgende dag reageren zou.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag erna zou reageren.","De minister beloofde dat hij de dag erna reageren zou."]},{"nl":"Bij de explosie zouden tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","en":"Ten people are said to have been injured in the explosion.","hint":["explosie","zullen","mens","gewond raken"],"note":"zouden marks a report that is not confirmed. For something in the past it is zou(den) + zijn/hebben + participle: zouden … gewond zijn geraakt.","alt":["Bij de explosie zouden tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn.","Tien mensen zouden bij de explosie gewond zijn geraakt.","Tien mensen zouden bij de explosie gewond geraakt zijn.","Bij de explosie zouden er tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","Bij de explosie zouden er tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn.","Er zouden bij de explosie tien mensen gewond zijn geraakt.","Er zouden bij de explosie tien mensen gewond geraakt zijn."]},{"nl":"Volgens een anonieme bron wist de redactie al maanden van de fraude.","en":"According to an anonymous source the editorial staff had known about the fraud for months.","hint":["volgens","anoniem","bron","redactie","weten","fraude"],"note":"Volgens een anonieme bron is the first element, so the verb wist comes straight after it, before the subject de redactie. Al maanden + past: had known for months.","alt":["De redactie wist volgens een anonieme bron al maanden van de fraude.","Volgens een anonieme bron had de redactie al maanden van de fraude geweten."]},{"nl":"De verslaggever had de bron naar eigen zeggen nooit ontmoet.","en":"By his own account, the reporter had never met the source.","hint":["verslaggever","bron","naar eigen zeggen","nooit","ontmoeten"],"note":"naar eigen zeggen says that only he himself claims this. It stands in the middle like an adverb, or first: Naar eigen zeggen had de verslaggever …","alt":["Naar eigen zeggen had de verslaggever de bron nooit ontmoet."]},{"nl":"Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister zou aftreden.","en":"She asked the spokesperson whether the minister would resign.","hint":["vragen","woordvoerder","minister","aftreden"],"note":"A yes/no question is reported with of. The question was about the future, so it becomes zou + infinitive at the end of the clause.","alt":["Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister af zou treden.","Ze vroeg de woordvoerder of de minister aftreden zou.","Ze vroeg aan de woordvoerder of de minister zou aftreden."]},{"nl":"De redactie zei dat ik het artikel moest inkorten.","en":"The editors said that I had to shorten the article.","hint":["redactie","zeggen","artikel","moeten","inkorten"],"note":"The order 'Kort het artikel in!' is reported as dat … moest + infinitive. In the clause the separable verb may stay whole (moest inkorten) or split (in moest korten).","alt":["De redactie zei dat ik het artikel in moest korten.","De redactie zei dat ik het artikel inkorten moest."]},{"nl":"De minister gaf toe dat hij met de verslaggever had gesproken.","en":"The minister admitted that he had spoken to the reporter.","hint":["minister","toegeven","verslaggever","spreken"],"note":"toegeven: he admits something he would rather not say. In the main clause the separable verb splits: gaf … toe; the perfect in the clause becomes had gesproken.","alt":["De minister gaf toe dat hij met de verslaggever gesproken had.","De minister gaf toe dat hij de verslaggever had gesproken.","De minister gaf toe dat hij de verslaggever gesproken had."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Volgens de woordvoerder ___ geen sprake van fraude.","options":["er is","is er","er"],"correct":1,"en":"According to the spokesperson there is no question of fraud.","note":"Volgens de woordvoerder is the first element, so the verb comes next: is er. *Volgens de woordvoerder er is breaks the verb-second rule."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De verslaggever vroeg ___ de minister het persbericht had gelezen.","options":["dat","als","of","wat"],"correct":2,"en":"The reporter asked whether the minister had read the press release.","note":"A reported yes/no question starts with of. Dat would make it a statement, and als for 'whether' is not standard Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Bij de brand ___ drie mensen gewond zijn geraakt, maar de politie heeft dat nog niet bevestigd.","options":["zou","werden","zouden"],"correct":2,"en":"Three people are said to have been injured in the fire, but the police have not confirmed that yet.","note":"zou(den) marks an unconfirmed report. The subject drie mensen is plural, so zouden; werden does not go with zijn geraakt."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De krant ___ dat de minister iets te verbergen had, maar schreef het niet letterlijk.","options":["benadrukte","suggereerde","gaf toe"],"correct":1,"en":"The newspaper implied that the minister had something to hide, but did not write it in so many words.","note":"suggereren is saying something without saying it outright. Benadrukken (to stress) does not fit with 'niet letterlijk', and a newspaper cannot admit something on the minister's behalf."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vorige week beweerde de politicus dat hij ziek ___ (zijn), maar die avond stond hij gewoon op een feest.","answers":["was"],"en":"Last week the politician claimed that he was ill, but that evening he was simply at a party.","note":"After the past verb beweerde, 'ik ben ziek' becomes was. Beweren also shows that the claim was doubtful."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Op maandag zei de woordvoerder dat de minister de volgende dag een verklaring ___ (zullen) afleggen.","answers":["zou"],"en":"On Monday the spokesperson said that the minister would make a statement the next day.","note":"A future reported after a past verb becomes zou + infinitive. Zal belongs to the moment of speaking, which lies in the past."},{"type":"gap","nl":"'Ik bel je morgen,' zei de bron vorige week. Hij zei dat hij me ___ zou bellen.","answers":["de volgende dag","de dag erna","de dag daarna"],"en":"He said that he would call me the next day.","note":"Morgen was seen from last week, so it becomes de volgende dag (or de dag erna). Morgen now would mean a different day."},{"type":"gap","nl":"'Ik heb de bron gisteren gesproken.' Ze vertelde dat ze de bron de dag ervoor ___ (hebben) gesproken.","answers":["had"],"en":"She said that she had spoken to the source the day before.","note":"The perfect heb gesproken becomes the past perfect had gesproken after vertelde, and gisteren becomes de dag ervoor."},{"type":"order","lead":"Volgens de politie","words":["zijn","er","bij de brand","twee mensen","gewond geraakt"],"answers":["Volgens de politie zijn er bij de brand twee mensen gewond geraakt.","Volgens de politie zijn er twee mensen bij de brand gewond geraakt.","Volgens de politie zijn er twee mensen gewond geraakt bij de brand."],"en":"According to the police two people were injured in the fire.","note":"After Volgens de politie comes the verb zijn, then er. Bij de brand may stand before or after twee mensen, or after the participle."},{"type":"order","lead":"De verslaggever vroeg","words":["of","de woordvoerder","het nieuws","kon","bevestigen"],"answers":["De verslaggever vroeg of de woordvoerder het nieuws kon bevestigen.","De verslaggever vroeg of de woordvoerder het nieuws bevestigen kon."],"en":"The reporter asked whether the spokesperson could confirm the news.","note":"of starts the reported question and the verbs go to the end. Kon bevestigen is the usual order; bevestigen kon is also correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"The spokesperson (he) said this. Report it with ontkennen: start with De woordvoerder ontkende dat.","from":"Ik heb het persbericht niet geschreven.","answers":["De woordvoerder ontkende dat hij het persbericht had geschreven.","De woordvoerder ontkende dat hij het persbericht geschreven had."],"en":"The spokesperson denied that he had written the press release.","note":"ontkennen already contains the 'not', so niet goes. With niet the sentence would mean that he claims he did write it. Heb geschreven becomes had geschreven."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"A journalist asked the spokesperson (he) this. Report it: start with De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder.","from":"Waarom heeft u de bron niet genoemd?","answers":["De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder waarom hij de bron niet had genoemd.","De journalist vroeg de woordvoerder waarom hij de bron niet genoemd had."],"en":"The journalist asked the spokesperson why he had not named the source.","note":"The question word waarom stays and starts the clause; u becomes hij, and heeft genoemd becomes had genoemd at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"The press officer said this to us. Report it later, somewhere else: start with De persvoorlichter vroeg ons, and use te.","from":"Wacht hier, alstublieft.","answers":["De persvoorlichter vroeg ons daar te wachten.","De persvoorlichter vroeg ons om daar te wachten."],"en":"The press officer asked us to wait there.","note":"A request becomes vragen + (om) te + infinitive. We are no longer in that place, so hier becomes daar."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with zou instead of naar verluidt. Start with De minister.","from":"Naar verluidt heeft de minister gelogen.","answers":["De minister zou hebben gelogen.","De minister zou gelogen hebben."],"en":"The minister allegedly lied.","note":"For an unconfirmed claim about the past use zou + hebben + participle. Both orders of hebben and gelogen are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Volgens de redactie de bewering klopt niet.","answers":["Volgens de redactie klopt de bewering niet."],"en":"According to the editors the claim is not correct.","note":"Volgens de redactie fills the first place, so the verb klopt must come second, before the subject de bewering."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het onderzoek is afgerond, aldus zei de woordvoerder.","answers":["Het onderzoek is afgerond, aldus de woordvoerder.","Het onderzoek is afgerond, zei de woordvoerder."],"en":"The investigation has been completed, according to the spokesperson.","note":"aldus + source already means 'so says': it takes no verb. Either aldus de woordvoerder or zei de woordvoerder, not both."}]},
  {"id":"b2-13","level":"B2","icon":"💹","title":"Money and the economy","grammar":"Infinitive clauses: door te, zonder te, na te hebben","summary":"door / zonder / in plaats van / alvorens / om … te + infinitive, and na … te hebben or te zijn + participle: a short clause with the subject of the main clause. Another subject needs doordat, zonder dat, nadat.","explain":"News and formal Dutch often use a short clause without a subject of its own, built on `te` + infinitive: `door te sparen`, `zonder te betalen`, `na te hebben gekeken`. Dutch grammar calls it a *shortened sub-clause* (*de beknopte bijzin*). It can leave the subject out because the doer is **the subject of the main clause**.\n\n### The words that start it\n\n| word | meaning | example |\n| door … te | by doing | door te sparen |\n| zonder … te | without doing | zonder te betalen |\n| in plaats van … te | instead of doing | in plaats van te lenen |\n| alvorens … te | before doing (formal) | alvorens te tekenen |\n| om … te | in order to | om te investeren |\n| na … te hebben / te zijn | after doing | na te hebben gelezen |\n\nEverything that belongs to the verb comes between the first word and `te`; `te` + infinitive closes the clause. A separable verb takes `te` between its parts (unit B1 · 17): `zonder iets uit te geven`.\n\n> Door minder uit te geven, betaalde ze haar schulden af. — By spending less, she paid off her debts.\n> Hij tekende het contract zonder de kleine letters te lezen. — He signed the contract without reading the small print.\n> In plaats van te bezuinigen, nam de onderneming meer personeel aan. — Instead of cutting costs, the business took on more staff.\n\n### na: always te hebben or te zijn\n\nAfter `na` the action is finished, so Dutch uses the perfect: `te hebben` or `te zijn` + participle, with the auxiliary of the perfect (`heeft gelezen`, `is gegaan`). The participle may stand before or after `te hebben`; both are correct.\n\n> Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij te verkopen. — After looking at the figures, he decided to sell.\n> Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd het bedrijf overgenomen. — After going bankrupt, the company was taken over.\n\n### om: required, optional or wrong\n\n- **Purpose** (in order to): `om` is required. `Ze leent geld om een huis te kopen`.\n- **After te + adjective**: also `om`: `Die aandelen zijn te riskant om te kopen`.\n- **After proberen, besluiten, beloven, vergeten, weigeren**: optional. `Hij besloot (om) te verkopen`: both are correct.\n- **After schijnen, lijken, blijken, hoeven** (next unit): never. `U hoeft niets te betalen`.\n\n### zijn, hebben, vallen, staan, geven + te\n\n- `zijn te`: can be done. `Dat is niet te betalen` (that is unaffordable); `Het is te hopen dat …` (it is to be hoped that …).\n- `hebben te`: have something to do. `Ik heb nog veel te doen`; `Je hebt niets te vrezen`.\n- `vallen te`, often with `er`: `Er valt weinig te verdienen`; `Dat valt nog te bezien` (that remains to be seen).\n- `staan te wachten`: lie ahead. `Ons staat een moeilijk jaar te wachten`.\n- `te denken geven`: `Die cijfers geven te denken` (those figures are a cause for concern).\n\n### The noun variant: door het + infinitive\n\n> Door het verhogen van de rente wil de centrale bank de inflatie remmen. — By raising interest rates the central bank wants to curb inflation.\n\n### Another subject: a full sub-clause\n\nWith `door`, `zonder`, `in plaats van`, `na` and `alvorens` the short clause only works when the doer is the subject of the main clause. Otherwise use a sub-clause with the verb at the end: `doordat`, `zonder dat`, `nadat`, `voordat`, `in plaats van dat`.\n\n> De koers daalde zonder dat iemand het had verwacht. — The share price fell without anyone having expected it.\n\n### Where the clause goes\n\nFirst or last. First, it fills the first place, so the conjugated verb follows and then the subject: `Door te sparen kun je later beleggen`. Last, it comes after the final verbs: `Hij heeft veel verloren door slecht te beleggen`. A comma after a clause at the start is common but not required. A short one can stand in the middle (`Hij is zonder te betalen vertrokken`), but first or last is the safe choice.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Na de cijfers te bekijken → `Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken`.\n- *Voordat te tekenen does not exist: `alvorens te tekenen` or `voordat je tekent`.\n- Another subject: *Door de koers te dalen → `Doordat de koers daalde`.\n- *Hij spaart een huis te kopen → `om een huis te kopen`.\n- *zonder te overnemen → `zonder over te nemen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian often uses an adverbial participle (дієприслівник): `Переглянувши цифри, він вирішив продати`. Dutch has no such form: it uses `na` + `te hebben` + participle, or `door` / `zonder` + `te` + infinitive.","words":[{"nl":"aandeel","art":"het","pl":"aandelen","en":"share (in a company)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aandeelhouder","art":"de","pl":"aandeelhouders","en":"shareholder","pos":"n"},{"nl":"belegger","art":"de","pl":"beleggers","en":"investor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beleggen","past":"belegde","perf":"heeft belegd","en":"to invest","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verlies","art":"het","pl":"verliezen","en":"loss","pos":"n"},{"nl":"concurrent","art":"de","pl":"concurrenten","en":"competitor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"koers","art":"de","pl":"koersen","en":"share price / exchange rate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"investering","art":"de","pl":"investeringen","en":"investment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezuinigen","past":"bezuinigde","perf":"heeft bezuinigd","en":"to cut costs / to economise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overnemen","past":"nam over","perf":"heeft overgenomen","en":"to take over (a company)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"onderneming","art":"de","pl":"ondernemingen","en":"business / enterprise","pos":"n"},{"nl":"winstgevend","en":"profitable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"failliet","en":"bankrupt","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"prijsverhoging","art":"de","pl":"prijsverhogingen","en":"price increase","pos":"n"},{"nl":"consument","art":"de","pl":"consumenten","en":"consumer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inflatie","art":"de","pl":"","en":"inflation","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Door elke maand een vast bedrag te beleggen, spreid je het risico.","en":"By investing a fixed amount every month, you spread the risk.","hint":["door","vast","bedrag","beleggen","spreiden","risico"],"note":"door … te + infinitive says how: the doer is je, the subject of the main clause. The clause stands first, so the verb spreid comes next, then je (no t: spreid je).","alt":["Je spreidt het risico door elke maand een vast bedrag te beleggen."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf heeft de concurrent overgenomen zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken.","en":"The company took over the competitor without examining the risks properly.","hint":["bedrijf","concurrent","overnemen","zonder","risico","onderzoeken"],"note":"English -ing after without becomes zonder … te + infinitive. The clause comes after the final verb overgenomen; the object de risico's stands between zonder and te.","alt":["Het bedrijf nam de concurrent over zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken.","Zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken, heeft het bedrijf de concurrent overgenomen.","Zonder de risico's goed te onderzoeken, nam het bedrijf de concurrent over."]},{"nl":"Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot de belegger zijn aandelen te verkopen.","en":"After looking at the figures, the investor decided to sell his shares.","hint":["na","cijfer","bekijken","besluiten","belegger","aandeel"],"note":"After na the looking is finished, so te hebben + participle. After besluiten om is optional: besloot (om) zijn aandelen te verkopen.","alt":["Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot de belegger zijn aandelen te verkopen.","Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot de belegger om zijn aandelen te verkopen.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot de belegger om zijn aandelen te verkopen."]},{"nl":"Na failliet te zijn gegaan, werd de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","en":"After going bankrupt, the business was taken over by a competitor.","hint":["na","failliet","gaan","onderneming","concurrent","overnemen"],"note":"Gaan takes zijn in the perfect (is failliet gegaan), so the na-clause has te zijn. Its doer is de onderneming, the subject of the main clause.","alt":["Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd de onderneming door een concurrent overgenomen.","Na failliet te zijn gegaan, werd de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent.","Na failliet gegaan te zijn, werd de onderneming overgenomen door een concurrent."]},{"nl":"In plaats van te bezuinigen, leende de onderneming nog meer geld.","en":"Instead of cutting costs, the business borrowed even more money.","hint":["in plaats van","bezuinigen","onderneming","lenen","geld"],"note":"in plaats van … te + infinitive. The clause fills the first place, so leende comes straight after it and the subject follows.","alt":["De onderneming leende nog meer geld in plaats van te bezuinigen.","In plaats van te bezuinigen, heeft de onderneming nog meer geld geleend.","De onderneming heeft nog meer geld geleend in plaats van te bezuinigen."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf leende geld om een nieuwe fabriek te bouwen.","en":"The company borrowed money to build a new factory.","hint":["bedrijf","lenen","geld","nieuw","fabriek","bouwen"],"note":"Purpose (in order to) always needs om … te. Without om the sentence is wrong.","alt":["Het bedrijf heeft geld geleend om een nieuwe fabriek te bouwen."]},{"nl":"Door de inflatie is een eigen huis voor veel consumenten niet meer te betalen.","en":"Because of inflation, a home of their own is no longer affordable for many consumers.","hint":["door","inflatie","eigen","huis","consument","betalen"],"note":"zijn + te + infinitive means that something can (or here cannot) be done: is niet te betalen. Door here is an ordinary preposition before a noun, without te.","alt":["Een eigen huis is door de inflatie voor veel consumenten niet meer te betalen.","Door de inflatie is een eigen huis niet meer te betalen voor veel consumenten."]},{"nl":"Kleine beleggers hebben voorlopig niets te vrezen.","en":"Small investors have nothing to fear for the time being.","hint":["klein","belegger","voorlopig","niets","vrezen"],"note":"hebben + te + infinitive: have something to do (here: nothing to fear). The infinitive with te goes to the end.","alt":["Voorlopig hebben kleine beleggers niets te vrezen."]},{"nl":"Over de gevolgen van de overname valt nog weinig te zeggen.","en":"Little can be said yet about the consequences of the takeover.","hint":["gevolg","overname","vallen","weinig","zeggen"],"note":"vallen + te + infinitive: something can (hardly) be done. When another part stands first, er is optional; when nothing else stands first, er fills that place: Er valt nog weinig te zeggen.","alt":["Over de gevolgen van de overname valt er nog weinig te zeggen.","Er valt nog weinig te zeggen over de gevolgen van de overname.","Er valt over de gevolgen van de overname nog weinig te zeggen."]},{"nl":"De directeur verkocht zijn aandelen zonder dat de aandeelhouders het wisten.","en":"The director sold his shares without the shareholders knowing.","hint":["directeur","verkopen","aandeel","zonder dat","aandeelhouder","weten"],"note":"The knowing is done by the shareholders, not by the director, so zonder … te is impossible: it has to be zonder dat with a full sub-clause and the verb at the end.","alt":["De directeur heeft zijn aandelen verkocht zonder dat de aandeelhouders het wisten.","De directeur verkocht zijn aandelen zonder dat de aandeelhouders ervan wisten.","De directeur heeft zijn aandelen verkocht zonder dat de aandeelhouders ervan wisten."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de prijzen te verhogen, maakte het bedrijf meer winst.","options":["Doordat","Na","Door"],"correct":2,"en":"By raising its prices, the company made more profit.","note":"Door … te + infinitive: by doing. Doordat needs a full sub-clause with its own subject and verb, and na needs te hebben + participle (na de prijzen te hebben verhoogd)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het bedrijf leent geld ___ een nieuwe winkel te openen.","options":["om","–","voor"],"correct":0,"en":"The company is borrowing money to open a new shop.","note":"Purpose needs om … te. Leaving om out is wrong, and voor … te is Flemish, not standard Netherlands Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Voor dit advies hoeft u niets ___ te betalen.","options":["om","voor","–"],"correct":2,"en":"You (formal) do not have to pay anything for this advice.","note":"After hoeven (and schijnen, lijken, blijken) te follows directly; om is never used there."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na jarenlang verlies te ___ geleden, is de onderneming nu eindelijk winstgevend.","options":["zijn","hebben","worden"],"correct":1,"en":"After making a loss for years, the business is finally profitable now.","note":"Verlies lijden takes hebben in the perfect (heeft verlies geleden), so the na-clause has te hebben. Te zijn is for verbs that take zijn, such as gaan or vertrekken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze willen de concurrent kopen zonder alle schulden ___ (overnemen).","answers":["over te nemen"],"en":"They want to buy the competitor without taking over all its debts.","note":"Overnemen is separable, so te goes between the parts: over te nemen, written as three words."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Deze investering ___ (vallen) niet binnen vijf jaar terug te verdienen.","answers":["valt"],"en":"This investment cannot be earned back within five years.","note":"vallen + te + infinitive means that something can or cannot be done. De investering is singular, so valt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Door de prijsverhogingen is benzine voor veel gezinnen bijna niet ___ (betalen).","answers":["te betalen"],"en":"Because of the price increases, petrol is almost unaffordable for many families.","note":"zijn + te + infinitive: is niet te betalen, it cannot be paid. Without te (is niet betalen) the sentence is wrong."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De lage koersen van dit jaar geven ___ (denken).","answers":["te denken"],"en":"This year's low share prices give food for thought.","note":"The fixed expression is te denken geven: something makes you worried or thoughtful. Te comes before the infinitive."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zonder de risico's te kennen,","words":["heeft","hij","al zijn geld","in aandelen","belegd"],"answers":["Zonder de risico's te kennen, heeft hij al zijn geld in aandelen belegd."],"en":"Without knowing the risks, he invested all his money in shares.","note":"The zonder-clause fills the first place, so the conjugated verb heeft follows, then the subject hij. The participle belegd goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het bedrijf leende geld","words":["om","de concurrent","over","te","nemen"],"answers":["Het bedrijf leende geld om de concurrent over te nemen."],"en":"The company borrowed money to take over the competitor.","note":"Om opens the clause, the object follows, and the separable verb closes it with te between its parts: over te nemen."},{"type":"order","lead":"De koers daalde","words":["zonder dat","de beleggers","het","hadden","verwacht"],"answers":["De koers daalde zonder dat de beleggers het hadden verwacht.","De koers daalde zonder dat de beleggers het verwacht hadden."],"en":"The share price fell without the investors having expected it.","note":"The investors are not the subject of the main clause, so it must be zonder dat with a full sub-clause. Both verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with door … te, starting with Door. Leave out zo.","from":"Het bedrijf bezuinigde op personeel. Zo voorkwam het een groot verlies.","answers":["Door op personeel te bezuinigen, voorkwam het bedrijf een groot verlies.","Door te bezuinigen op personeel, voorkwam het bedrijf een groot verlies."],"en":"By cutting staff costs, the company prevented a big loss.","note":"The company does both things, so door … te works. The clause stands first, so voorkwam comes next and then het bedrijf, which replaces het."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use na … te hebben instead of the nadat-clause.","from":"Nadat de belegger het jaarverslag had gelezen, kocht hij extra aandelen.","answers":["Na het jaarverslag te hebben gelezen, kocht de belegger extra aandelen.","Na het jaarverslag gelezen te hebben, kocht de belegger extra aandelen."],"en":"After reading the annual report, the investor bought extra shares.","note":"The na-clause has no subject, so de belegger moves into the main clause. Lezen takes hebben; te hebben may stand before or after gelezen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace the voordat-clause with alvorens … te.","from":"Voordat u belegt, moet u de risico's kennen.","answers":["Alvorens te beleggen, moet u de risico's kennen."],"en":"Before investing, you (formal) need to know the risks.","note":"Alvorens … te is the formal short form of a voordat-clause with the same subject. Voordat itself cannot take te."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Door de koers te dalen, verloren veel beleggers geld.","answers":["Doordat de koers daalde, verloren veel beleggers geld.","Omdat de koers daalde, verloren veel beleggers geld."],"en":"Because the share price fell, many investors lost money.","note":"The price falls, the investors lose: two different subjects. Door … te is only possible when the doer is the subject of the main clause, so it must be a full sub-clause: Doordat de koers daalde."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Na de cijfers te bekijken, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","answers":["Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot hij zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers te hebben bekeken, besloot hij om zijn aandelen te houden.","Na de cijfers bekeken te hebben, besloot hij om zijn aandelen te houden."],"en":"After looking at the figures, he decided to keep his shares.","note":"After na the action is finished, so Dutch uses te hebben + participle: na de cijfers te hebben bekeken. Na + te + a plain infinitive is not standard Dutch."}]},
  {"id":"b2-17","level":"B2","icon":"🎭","title":"Art and literature","grammar":"Referring back: hij or het, deze, die, zulke, de eerste","summary":"A de-word is hij / hem / zijn, a het-word het / zijn, a plural ze / hun. At the front: die, dat. hen after a preposition and as object, hun 'to them', never hun as subject. zo'n + singular, zulke + plural.","explain":"A good text does not repeat its nouns; it refers back to them. In Dutch the pronoun follows the **gender of the noun** (de or het), not the question whether it is a person or a thing. English says *it* for every thing; Dutch says `hij` for a film and `het` for a book. This unit puts the whole system together.\n\n### Things: hij, het, ze\n\n| noun | subject | object | possessive |\n| de film | hij | hem | zijn |\n| het boek | het | het | zijn |\n| de boeken | ze | ze | hun |\n\n> De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is erg lang. — The novel is moving, but it is very long.\n> Het museum toont een groot deel van zijn collectie. — The museum is showing a large part of its collection.\n\nThe possessive of a het-word is `zijn`, never *hun and not *haar: `het museum en zijn collectie`. `hun` is only for plurals. A person keeps natural gender: `de auteur` is hij or zij, and a het-word for a person usually gets the person's pronoun too: `Het meisje zegt dat ze moe is.`\n\n### Feminine reference: zij and haar\n\nIn formal writing, de-words for organisations (`de regering`, `de gemeente`) and abstract de-words in `-ing`, `-heid`, `-schap`, `-ie` and `-teit` are traditionally feminine: `de regering en haar plannen`. Recognise it when you read it. In the Netherlands `hij` / `zijn` is also accepted for these words and is what people say, so both are correct.\n\n### die and dat at the front\n\nWhen the pronoun takes the first place, usually to pick up what was just mentioned, Dutch uses `die` (de-words and plurals) and `dat` (het-words) instead of `hem` and `het`. An object `het` can never stand first.\n\n> Die film? Die heb ik vorige week al gezien. — That film? I already saw it last week.\n> Dat boek? Dat heb ik nog niet gelezen. — That book? I have not read it yet.\n\n### deze, de laatste, eerstgenoemde\n\nWhen `hij` could point at two people, written Dutch uses `deze`: the **last** person or thing mentioned. `de eerste … de laatste` and, more formally, `eerstgenoemde … laatstgenoemde` mean *the former … the latter*.\n\n> De auteur belde de uitgever. Deze vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek. — The author phoned the publisher. The latter did not find the main character likeable.\n\n### People in the plural: zij, ze, hen, hun\n\n- subject: `zij` or unstressed `ze`, **never** *hun hebben.\n- direct object: `hen` in writing, `ze` in speech: `Ik heb hen gezien.`\n- indirect object (to them): `hun` in writing, `ze` in speech: `Ik heb hun een kaartje gegeven.`\n- after a preposition: `hen`: `met hen`, `voor hen`, never *met hun. For things you use the er-word instead: `ermee`, `erover` (unit B1 · 05).\n\n> Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jaren samen met hen geëxposeerd. — I know those two artists well: I exhibited with them for years.\n\n### zo'n, zulke, zulk, dergelijk\n\n- `zo'n` + a singular noun you can count: `zo'n film`, `zo'n boek`.\n- `zulke` + a plural: `zulke films`; also before a de-word without plural: `zulke muziek`.\n- `zulk` + a het-word without plural: `zulk weer`, `zulk talent`.\n- `dergelijk` is the formal word and behaves like an adjective: `een dergelijke film`, `een dergelijk boek` (het-word after een: no -e), `dergelijke films`.\n- `zo iemand` (such a person) is two words, `zoiets` (something like that) one.\n\nBefore a number `zo'n` means *about*: `zo'n twintig doeken`. *zo'n mooie boeken is heard but wrong: `zulke mooie boeken`.\n\n### dezelfde and hetzelfde\n\n`dezelfde` goes with de-words and plurals, `hetzelfde` with het-words, each written as one word. On its own, `hetzelfde` means *the same thing*.\n\n> Ze vertellen hetzelfde verhaal, maar niet op dezelfde manier. — They tell the same story, but not in the same way.\n\n### het pointing forward; het or er\n\n`het` can announce a clause that follows. It cannot be left out: `Ik vind het jammer dat …`, never *Ik vind jammer dat ….\n\n> Het is opvallend dat het museum zo weinig bezoekers heeft. — It is striking that the museum has so few visitors.\n\n`het` is also the subject of weather and time (`Het regent`) and of sentences that name or identify, even with a plural: `Het zijn goede acteurs`. *There is / there are* with an indefinite subject is `er`: `Er is een nieuwe tentoonstelling`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also chooses він, вона, воно by grammatical gender, so the idea is familiar. But the genders do not match: `de roman` is hij like роман, but `het boek` is het while книга is вона. And `Шкода, що …` needs no 'it', while Dutch needs `het`.","words":[{"nl":"roman","art":"de","pl":"romans","en":"novel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"auteur","art":"de","pl":"auteurs","en":"author","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitgever","art":"de","pl":"uitgevers","en":"publisher","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hoofdpersoon","art":"de","pl":"hoofdpersonen","en":"main character","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toneelstuk","art":"het","pl":"toneelstukken","en":"play (in the theatre)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voorstelling","art":"de","pl":"voorstellingen","en":"performance / show","pos":"n"},{"nl":"tentoonstelling","art":"de","pl":"tentoonstellingen","en":"exhibition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kunstenaar","art":"de","pl":"kunstenaars","en":"artist","pos":"n"},{"nl":"collectie","art":"de","pl":"collecties","en":"collection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"meesterwerk","art":"het","pl":"meesterwerken","en":"masterpiece","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kunstwerk","art":"het","pl":"kunstwerken","en":"work of art","pos":"n"},{"nl":"doek","art":"het","pl":"doeken","en":"canvas / painting","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verschijnen","past":"verscheen","perf":"is verschenen","en":"to appear / to be published","pos":"v"},{"nl":"exposeren","past":"exposeerde","perf":"heeft geëxposeerd","en":"to exhibit","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verfilmen","past":"verfilmde","perf":"heeft verfilmd","en":"to make into a film","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontroerend","en":"moving / touching","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is erg lang.","en":"The novel is moving, but it is very long.","hint":["roman","ontroerend","erg","lang"],"note":"Roman is a de-word, so English 'it' becomes hij. Het would be wrong here: het only refers back to het-words.","alt":["De roman is ontroerend, maar hij is heel lang."]},{"nl":"Het toneelstuk is goed geschreven, maar het duurt te lang.","en":"The play is well written, but it goes on too long.","hint":["toneelstuk","goed","schrijven","duren","lang"],"note":"Toneelstuk is a het-word, so it is referred to with het. A de-word would have needed hij."},{"nl":"Die film? Die heb ik vorige week al gezien.","en":"That film? I already saw it last week.","hint":["film","zien","vorige week","al"],"note":"The pronoun takes the first place to pick up the topic, so a de-word gets die instead of hem. The verb heb stays second and ik follows it.","alt":["Die film? Die heb ik al vorige week gezien.","Die film? Ik heb die vorige week al gezien.","Die film? Ik heb hem vorige week al gezien."]},{"nl":"Het museum toont dit jaar een groot deel van zijn collectie.","en":"This year the museum is showing a large part of its collection.","hint":["museum","tonen","jaar","deel","collectie"],"note":"Museum is a het-word, and the possessive of a het-word is zijn. Hun is only for plurals, and haar does not go with a het-word.","alt":["Dit jaar toont het museum een groot deel van zijn collectie."]},{"nl":"De auteur belde de uitgever. Deze vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek.","en":"The author phoned the publisher. The latter did not find the main character likeable.","hint":["auteur","bellen","uitgever","hoofdpersoon","sympathiek"],"note":"Hij could mean the author or the publisher; deze points at the last one mentioned, the publisher. This is written style.","alt":["De auteur belde de uitgever. De laatste vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek.","De auteur belde de uitgever. Laatstgenoemde vond de hoofdpersoon niet sympathiek."]},{"nl":"Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik heb hen nergens gezien.","en":"I wanted to talk to the actors after the performance, but I did not see them anywhere.","hint":["willen","acteur","voorstelling","spreken","nergens","zien"],"note":"Them is the direct object of gezien, which is hen in written Dutch and ze in speech. Hun would be the indirect object (to them).","alt":["Ik wilde de acteurs na de voorstelling spreken, maar ik heb ze nergens gezien.","Ik wilde na de voorstelling de acteurs spreken, maar ik heb hen nergens gezien.","Ik wilde na de voorstelling de acteurs spreken, maar ik heb ze nergens gezien."]},{"nl":"Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jaren samen met hen geëxposeerd.","en":"I know those two artists well: I exhibited with them for years.","hint":["kennen","kunstenaar","jaar","exposeren"],"note":"After a preposition, people in the plural are hen: met hen. Never met hun. Geëxposeerd has a trema because ge and e belong to different syllables.","alt":["Ik ken die twee kunstenaars goed: ik heb jarenlang samen met hen geëxposeerd."]},{"nl":"Ik had nog nooit zulke grote doeken gezien.","en":"I had never seen such big canvases before.","hint":["nog nooit","groot","doek","zien"],"note":"Doeken is plural, so such is zulke. Zo'n only goes with a singular noun: zo'n groot doek.","alt":["Zulke grote doeken had ik nog nooit gezien."]},{"nl":"Het toneelstuk en de film vertellen hetzelfde verhaal, maar niet op dezelfde manier.","en":"The play and the film tell the same story, but not in the same way.","hint":["toneelstuk","film","vertellen","verhaal","manier"],"note":"Verhaal is a het-word: hetzelfde verhaal. Manier is a de-word: dezelfde manier. Both are written as one word."},{"nl":"Ik vind het jammer dat de roman nooit is verfilmd.","en":"I think it is a pity that the novel has never been made into a film.","hint":["vinden","jammer","roman","nooit","verfilmen"],"note":"Het announces the dat-clause that follows, and it cannot be left out: never 'Ik vind jammer dat'. At the end both orders are correct: is verfilmd and verfilmd is.","alt":["Ik vind het jammer dat de roman nooit verfilmd is."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De nieuwe roman is vorige week verschenen. ___ is echt heel spannend.","options":["Het","Hij","Ze"],"correct":1,"en":"The new novel came out last week. It is really very exciting.","note":"De roman is a de-word, so it is hij, although English says 'it'. Het is for het-words, ze for plurals."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het museum heeft ___ hele collectie online gezet.","options":["hun","haar","zijn","het"],"correct":2,"en":"The museum has put its whole collection online.","note":"Museum is a het-word, so its possessive is zijn. Hun is only for plurals, and 'het museum en hun collectie' is a common mistake even among native speakers."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ is deze maand een bijzondere tentoonstelling in het museum.","options":["Het","Die","Er"],"correct":2,"en":"There is a special exhibition in the museum this month.","note":"'There is' with an indefinite subject (een tentoonstelling) is er is. Het would say 'it is a special exhibition'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb nog nooit ___ mooie kunstwerken bij elkaar gezien.","options":["zo'n","zulke","zulk"],"correct":1,"en":"I have never seen such beautiful works of art together.","note":"Kunstwerken is plural, so zulke. Zo'n is for one countable thing (zo'n mooi kunstwerk), zulk for a het-word without plural (zulk weer)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De kunstenaars zaten in de zaal, en het hele publiek klapte voor ___.","options":["hen","zij","hun"],"correct":0,"en":"The artists were sitting in the hall, and the whole audience applauded them.","note":"After a preposition (voor), people in the plural are hen. Hun is only the indirect object without a preposition, and zij is a subject form."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wie staan er op de poster? ___ zijn de regisseur en de hoofdrolspeler.","options":["Er","Hun","Het"],"correct":2,"en":"Who is on the poster? They are the director and the lead actor.","note":"A sentence that identifies people starts with het, and the verb agrees with the plural: het zijn. Hun is never a subject, and er is for 'there is'. (Dat zijn … is also correct.)"},{"type":"gap","nl":"Die actrice speelt al tien jaar in ___ (the same) toneelstuk.","answers":["hetzelfde"],"en":"That actress has been acting in the same play for ten years.","note":"Toneelstuk is a het-word, so the same is hetzelfde, one word. With a de-word it would be dezelfde: dezelfde voorstelling."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Een ___ (dergelijk) meesterwerk zie je maar zelden.","answers":["dergelijk"],"en":"You only rarely see a masterpiece like that.","note":"Dergelijk behaves like an adjective. Meesterwerk is a het-word, and after een an adjective before a het-word has no -e: een dergelijk meesterwerk, but een dergelijke film."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Na afloop heb ik de acteurs gesproken en ___ (them) een compliment gegeven.","answers":["hun","ze"],"en":"Afterwards I spoke to the actors and gave them a compliment.","note":"The actors receive the compliment: indirect object, so hun in written Dutch, ze in speech. Hen is for the direct object and after a preposition."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De gemeente verlaagt volgend jaar ___ (its) subsidie aan het theater.","answers":["haar","zijn"],"en":"Next year the municipality is cutting its grant to the theatre.","note":"Gemeente is a de-word for an organisation. Formal writing traditionally treats it as feminine (haar); in the Netherlands zijn is also accepted. Hun is wrong: the municipality is one thing."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dat boek?","words":["Dat","heb","ik","nog niet","gelezen"],"answers":["Dat boek? Dat heb ik nog niet gelezen.","Dat boek? Ik heb dat nog niet gelezen."],"en":"That book? I have not read it yet.","note":"Dat picks up the het-word boek and usually takes the first place; then heb comes second and ik follows. An object het cannot stand first: never 'Het heb ik nog niet gelezen'."},{"type":"order","lead":"De regisseur sprak met de schrijver.","words":["Deze","vond","het einde","te somber"],"answers":["De regisseur sprak met de schrijver. Deze vond het einde te somber."],"en":"The director spoke to the writer. The latter thought the ending was too gloomy.","note":"Deze refers to the last person mentioned, de schrijver. Hij would leave it open who found the ending too gloomy."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace de roman with a pronoun.","from":"Ik heb de roman in één weekend gelezen.","answers":["Ik heb hem in één weekend gelezen.","Die heb ik in één weekend gelezen.","Ik heb die in één weekend gelezen."],"en":"I read it in one weekend.","note":"Roman is a de-word, so the object pronoun is hem, not het. At the front of the sentence Dutch prefers die."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Het is jammer.","from":"Dat de première is uitgesteld, is jammer.","answers":["Het is jammer dat de première is uitgesteld.","Het is jammer dat de première uitgesteld is."],"en":"It is a pity that the premiere has been postponed.","note":"Het stands in the subject place and points forward to the dat-clause, which now comes at the end. Both orders of is and uitgesteld are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het kunstwerk werd in 1990 gestolen, maar de politie heeft hem nooit teruggevonden.","answers":["Het kunstwerk werd in 1990 gestolen, maar de politie heeft het nooit teruggevonden."],"en":"The work of art was stolen in 1990, but the police never found it again.","note":"Kunstwerk is a het-word, so the object pronoun is het, not hem. Hem is for de-words: de film … hem."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want hun hebben de voorstelling al gezien.","answers":["Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want ze hebben de voorstelling al gezien.","Mijn ouders gaan niet mee, want zij hebben de voorstelling al gezien."],"en":"My parents are not coming along, because they have already seen the show.","note":"Hun is never a subject. The subject is zij or, unstressed, ze. 'Hun hebben' is heard, but it is a well-known mistake."}]},
  {"id":"b2-21","level":"B2","icon":"🏰","title":"History and tradition","grammar":"Fixed formal phrases: old case forms and the subjunctive","summary":"Old case forms live on in fixed phrases: 's avonds, des te beter, ten slotte, ter ere van, wiens / wier. The old subjunctive lives on in wishes: Leve de koning!, Het ga je goed, Moge … Learn each one whole; never build new ones.","explain":"Old Dutch had cases, as Ukrainian still has, and a verb form for wishes, the subjunctive (*de aanvoegende wijs*). Modern Dutch has lost both, but they survive in fixed phrases. Some are everyday Dutch, others belong to ceremonies and laws. You do not build them: you learn each one as one piece.\n\n### The old genitive: 's, des, der\n\n`'s` is short for `des`, the old 'of the' (the genitive, *de tweede naamval*) for masculine and neuter words; `der` was the form for feminine and plural words.\n\n| phrase | meaning | where |\n| 's morgens, 's avonds, 's nachts | in the morning, evening, at night | everyday |\n| 's zomers, 's winters | in summer, in winter | everyday |\n| een dezer dagen | one of these days | everyday |\n| hoe …, des te … | the …, the … | everyday |\n| 's werelds oudste | the world's oldest | news |\n| het Koninkrijk der Nederlanden | the Kingdom of the Netherlands | official |\n| de heer des huizes | the master of the house | ceremonial, jokey |\n\n> 's Avonds is het paleis verlicht. — In the evening the palace is lit up.\n> Hoe ouder het gebouw is, des te strenger zijn de regels. — The older the building, the stricter the rules.\n\n`'s` is always lower case; at the start of a sentence the next word gets the capital: `'s Avonds`. Place names add a hyphen: `'s-Hertogenbosch` (the duke's forest).\n\n`wiens` and `wier` are formal 'whose': `wiens` after a masculine or neuter singular word, `wier` after a feminine word or a plural. In speech: `van wie` (unit B2 · 8).\n\n> Het zijn families wier namen iedereen kent. — They are families whose names everyone knows.\n\n### The old dative: ten and ter\n\nAfter `te` the old dative article (*de derde naamval*) merged with it: `ten` = te + den (masculine and neuter), `ter` = te + der (feminine). Modern Dutch no longer tells masculine from feminine de-words, so you cannot work out which one a phrase takes: `ten tijde van` but `ter wereld`. Learn them as they are.\n\n- **Everyday**: `ten eerste` (firstly), `ten slotte` (finally), `ten minste` (at least), `ter plaatse` (on the spot), `ter wereld` (in the world), `van harte` (sincerely), `met name` (especially).\n- **Formal**: `ten gevolge van` (as a result of), `ten behoeve van` (for the benefit of), `ten opzichte van` (compared with), `ter gelegenheid van` (on the occasion of), `ter ere van` (in honour of), `ter attentie van` (for the attention of), `te allen tijde` (at all times).\n- **Ceremonial**: `in dier voege` (in such a way), `in den beginne` (in the beginning).\n\n> Ter ere van de bevrijding is er een concert. — There is a concert in honour of the liberation.\n\nTwo pairs differ in meaning. `ten minste` = not less than; `tenminste` = at least, that is (correcting yourself). `ten slotte` = finally; `tenslotte` = after all. People mix them up, but the official word list keeps them apart.\n\n### The subjunctive: wishes in set formulas\n\nThe old subjunctive is the stem + `-e` (of `zijn`: `zij`). Only fixed formulas survive:\n\n| formula | meaning |\n| Leve de koning! / Lang leve … | Long live the king! |\n| Het ga je goed! | All the best! |\n| God zij dank. | Thank God. |\n| Moge het nieuwe jaar … | May the new year … |\n| koste wat het kost | at any price |\n| als het ware / zij het | as it were / albeit |\n| hetzij … hetzij / het zij zo | either … or / so be it |\n| Wat er ook gebeure / Men neme … | Whatever may happen / Take … |\n\n`Als het ware`, `koste wat het kost` (also `koste wat kost`), `God zij dank` and `Het ga je goed` are everyday Dutch, and `Leve` is used freely: `Leve de vakantie!` `Moge`, `zij het` and `hetzij … hetzij` are formal; `Men neme` (old recipes) and `Wat er ook gebeure` sound old or jokey. `dankzij` was once `dank zij`, 'thanks be': so it only goes with a good cause.\n\n> Moge dit monument ons altijd aan de oorlog herinneren. — May this monument always remind us of the war.\n> Het was een succes, zij het een klein succes. — It was a success, albeit a small one.\n\n### Word order\n\nEach phrase is one element; in first place the verb follows it: `Ten slotte legde de koning een krans`. After `des te` + comparative the verb follows too. A `wat … ook` clause stands outside the sentence, and the main clause keeps its normal order: `Wat er ook gebeurt, wij blijven.`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Dankzij de storm is het beschadigd: a bad cause takes `door`, `vanwege` or `ten gevolge van`.\n- No new forms: not *Hij kome morgen or *ten auto, but `Hij komt morgen`, `met de auto`.\n- *de prinses, wiens moeder: for a woman it is `wier`.\n- `'s avonds`, never *s'avonds.\n- No t in a wish: `Het ga je goed!` *Het gaat je goed is a statement: 'you are doing well'.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`Хай живе король!` is `Leve de koning!`, but outside these formulas Dutch has no `хай` form: say `Ik hoop dat …`.","words":[{"nl":"vorstenhuis","art":"het","pl":"vorstenhuizen","en":"royal house / dynasty","pos":"n"},{"nl":"troon","art":"de","pl":"tronen","en":"throne","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inhuldiging","art":"de","pl":"inhuldigingen","en":"inauguration (of a monarch)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"staatshoofd","art":"het","pl":"staatshoofden","en":"head of state","pos":"n"},{"nl":"opvolger","art":"de","pl":"opvolgers","en":"successor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"regeren","past":"regeerde","perf":"heeft geregeerd","en":"to reign / to govern","pos":"v"},{"nl":"herdenking","art":"de","pl":"herdenkingen","en":"commemoration","pos":"n"},{"nl":"herdenken","past":"herdacht","perf":"heeft herdacht","en":"to commemorate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"gedenkteken","art":"het","pl":"gedenktekens","en":"memorial","pos":"n"},{"nl":"onthullen","past":"onthulde","perf":"heeft onthuld","en":"to unveil / to reveal","pos":"v"},{"nl":"plechtigheid","art":"de","pl":"plechtigheden","en":"ceremony","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plechtig","en":"solemn","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"bevrijding","art":"de","pl":"bevrijdingen","en":"liberation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"erfgoed","art":"het","pl":"","en":"heritage","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"overlevering","art":"de","pl":"overleveringen","en":"tradition (passed down) / legend","pos":"n"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ter gelegenheid van de inhuldiging was de hele stad oranje versierd.","en":"On the occasion of the inauguration the whole city was decorated in orange.","hint":["gelegenheid","inhuldiging","stad","oranje","versieren"],"note":"ter = te + der, the old dative of a feminine word (de gelegenheid). The whole phrase is one element in first place, so the verb was follows it.","alt":["De hele stad was ter gelegenheid van de inhuldiging oranje versierd."]},{"nl":"Op 4 mei herdenken we 's avonds de slachtoffers van de oorlog.","en":"On 4 May we commemorate the victims of the war in the evening.","hint":["4 mei","herdenken","avond","slachtoffer","oorlog"],"note":"'s avonds is the old genitive des avonds. The apostrophe stands before the s, and inside the sentence the word stays lower case.","alt":["We herdenken op 4 mei 's avonds de slachtoffers van de oorlog.","Op 4 mei 's avonds herdenken we de slachtoffers van de oorlog."]},{"nl":"Het gedenkteken werd ter ere van de gevallen soldaten onthuld.","en":"The memorial was unveiled in honour of the fallen soldiers.","hint":["gedenkteken","ere","gevallen","soldaat","onthullen"],"note":"ter ere van: ter + the old dative ere (from de eer). Never *ten ere or *te eer.","alt":["Het gedenkteken werd onthuld ter ere van de gevallen soldaten.","Ter ere van de gevallen soldaten werd het gedenkteken onthuld.","Het gedenkteken is ter ere van de gevallen soldaten onthuld.","Het gedenkteken is onthuld ter ere van de gevallen soldaten.","Ter ere van de gevallen soldaten is het gedenkteken onthuld."]},{"nl":"De koning, wiens opvolger nog een kind was, regeerde veertig jaar.","en":"The king, whose successor was still a child, reigned for forty years.","hint":["koning","opvolger","kind","regeren","jaar"],"note":"wiens is formal 'whose' for a masculine word: de koning. In speech you would also hear van wie de opvolger.","alt":["De koning, wiens opvolger nog een kind was, heeft veertig jaar geregeerd.","De koning, van wie de opvolger nog een kind was, regeerde veertig jaar."]},{"nl":"Dit is een van de oudste vorstenhuizen ter wereld.","en":"This is one of the oldest royal houses in the world.","hint":["oud","vorstenhuis","wereld"],"note":"ter wereld is the fixed phrase for 'in the world' after a superlative. The genitive 's werelds oudste vorstenhuizen says the same in the style of the news.","alt":["Dit is een van 's werelds oudste vorstenhuizen.","Dit is een van de oudste vorstenhuizen van de wereld.","Dit is één van de oudste vorstenhuizen ter wereld."]},{"nl":"Moge het nieuwe jaar u veel geluk en gezondheid brengen.","en":"May the new year bring you (formal) much happiness and health.","hint":["mogen","nieuw","jaar","geluk","gezondheid","brengen"],"note":"Moge is the old subjunctive of mogen: a wish, typical of greeting cards and speeches. It stands first, and brengen goes to the end."},{"nl":"Ten gevolge van de oorlog werd de plechtigheid een jaar uitgesteld.","en":"As a result of the war (ten …) the ceremony was postponed for a year.","hint":["gevolg","oorlog","plechtigheid","jaar","uitstellen"],"note":"ten = te + den, the old dative of a neuter word (het gevolg). Ten gevolge van is the formal way to name a cause, often a bad one.","alt":["De plechtigheid werd ten gevolge van de oorlog een jaar uitgesteld.","Ten gevolge van de oorlog is de plechtigheid een jaar uitgesteld.","De plechtigheid is ten gevolge van de oorlog een jaar uitgesteld."]},{"nl":"Dankzij de bevrijding werd het feest na vijf jaar weer gevierd.","en":"Thanks to the liberation the festival was celebrated again after five years.","hint":["dankzij","bevrijding","feest","jaar","vieren"],"note":"dankzij comes from dank zij, 'thanks be': it is only used for a good cause, like the liberation.","alt":["Dankzij de bevrijding werd het feest na vijf jaar opnieuw gevierd.","Het feest werd dankzij de bevrijding na vijf jaar weer gevierd.","Dankzij de bevrijding is het feest na vijf jaar weer gevierd."]},{"nl":"Het staatshoofd was zelf ter plaatse om de nabestaanden te steunen.","en":"The head of state was on the spot in person to support the bereaved.","hint":["staatshoofd","zelf","plaats","nabestaande","steunen"],"note":"ter plaatse: ter because de plaats was feminine, plaatse with the old dative -e. It means 'there, where it happened', a phrase of the news.","alt":["Het staatshoofd was zelf ter plekke om de nabestaanden te steunen."]},{"nl":"Ons erfgoed moet te allen tijde beschermd worden.","en":"Our heritage must be protected at all times.","hint":["erfgoed","moeten","allen","tijd","beschermen"],"note":"te allen tijde keeps two old dative endings, allen and tijde. It belongs to formal writing; in speech you say altijd.","alt":["Ons erfgoed moet te allen tijde worden beschermd."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Hoe plechtiger de ceremonie is, ___ te stiller wordt het publiek.","options":["des","de","dan","hoe"],"correct":0,"en":"The more solemn the ceremony, the quieter the audience becomes.","note":"des te is a fixed pair: des is the old genitive. You can say hoe … hoe stiller (without te), but before te only des fits. The verb follows straight after the comparative."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na de herdenking werd geld ingezameld ___ behoeve van de nabestaanden.","options":["ter","ten","te","voor"],"correct":1,"en":"After the commemoration money was collected for the benefit of the bereaved.","note":"The fixed phrase is ten behoeve van. You cannot work out ten or ter from modern de and het; learn the phrase whole."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de storm is het oude gedenkteken zwaar beschadigd.","options":["Dankzij","Ter ere van","Door"],"correct":2,"en":"Because of the storm the old memorial has been badly damaged.","note":"Damage is a bad result, so door. Dankzij (from 'thanks be') only goes with a good cause."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De troon is ___ driehonderd jaar oud.","options":["tenminste","ten minste","ter minste"],"correct":1,"en":"The throne is at least three hundred years old.","note":"'Not less than' is ten minste, two words. Tenminste, one word, corrects what you just said: 'at least, that is what I think'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We moeten het oude centrum beschermen; het is ___ ons erfgoed.","options":["ten slotte","ter slotte","tenslotte"],"correct":2,"en":"We must protect the old centre; it is our heritage, after all.","note":"'After all' is tenslotte, one word. Ten slotte, two words, means 'finally, as the last step'. Ter slotte does not exist."},{"type":"choose","nl":"U kunt de kaartjes voor het paleis ___ online, hetzij aan de kassa kopen.","options":["het zij","zowel","of","hetzij"],"correct":3,"en":"You can buy the tickets for the palace either online or at the ticket office.","note":"hetzij … hetzij is a fixed formal pair: either … or. The second hetzij needs a first one; zowel would need als, and of … of would need of twice."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Volgens de overlevering verscheen de geest van de graaf 's ___ (nacht) in het kasteel.","answers":["nachts"],"en":"According to tradition the count's ghost appeared in the castle at night.","note":"'s nachts is the old genitive des nachts, so the noun gets an s: nacht becomes nachts."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (gaan) je goed in je nieuwe land!","answers":["ga"],"en":"All the best in your new country!","note":"A wish in the old subjunctive: the stem + e, which for gaan is ga. Het gaat je goed would be a statement: 'you are doing well'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Lang ___ (leven) de koningin!","answers":["leve"],"en":"Long live the queen!","note":"leve is the subjunctive: the stem leef + e, with f becoming v. No t: it is a wish, not a statement."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De koningen, ___ portretten hier hangen, hebben allemaal lang geregeerd.","answers":["wier"],"en":"The kings, whose portraits hang here, all reigned for a long time.","note":"'whose' after a plural is wier. wiens is only for a masculine or neuter singular word."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ten slotte","words":["legde","het staatshoofd","een krans","bij het gedenkteken"],"answers":["Ten slotte legde het staatshoofd een krans bij het gedenkteken.","Ten slotte legde het staatshoofd bij het gedenkteken een krans."],"en":"Finally the head of state laid a wreath at the memorial.","note":"Ten slotte (finally) is one element in first place, so the verb legde comes next and then the subject."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it formal: replace Door with ten gevolge van.","from":"Door de brand was het paleis maandenlang gesloten.","answers":["Ten gevolge van de brand was het paleis maandenlang gesloten."],"en":"As a result of the fire the palace was closed for months.","note":"Ten gevolge van takes the place of door as one element, so the inversion stays: was het paleis."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it in everyday Dutch: replace the old subjunctive by the present tense.","from":"Wat er ook gebeure, de traditie blijft.","answers":["Wat er ook gebeurt, de traditie blijft."],"en":"Whatever happens, the tradition stays.","note":"gebeure is the old subjunctive; today it is gebeurt. The wat … ook clause stands outside the sentence, so the main clause keeps its normal order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn it into a ceremonial wish that starts with Moge.","from":"Wij hopen dat de nieuwe koning lang en wijs zal regeren.","answers":["Moge de nieuwe koning lang en wijs regeren!"],"en":"May the new king reign long and wisely!","note":"Moge is the subjunctive of mogen and takes the first place; the infinitive regeren goes to the end. The subject de nieuwe koning follows moge."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dankzij de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","answers":["Door de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Ten gevolge van de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Als gevolg van de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Vanwege de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld.","Wegens de oorlog werd de inhuldiging een jaar uitgesteld."],"en":"Because of the war the inauguration was postponed for a year.","note":"A war is not something to be thankful for, so dankzij is wrong. Use door, vanwege, wegens or the formal ten gevolge van."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De koningin, wiens zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis.","answers":["De koningin, wier zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis.","De koningin, van wie de zoon nu regeert, woont nog altijd in het paleis."],"en":"The queen, whose son now reigns, still lives in the palace.","note":"'whose' refers to de koningin, a woman, so it is wier. wiens is for a man or a masculine or neuter word."}]}
];
